WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.742
The answer to check two: the
manager drift would have been folded

2
00:00:03.742 --> 00:00:07.225
into the department one, and its
intended state never re-read.

3
00:00:07.225 --> 00:00:10.087
Scene three: who may touch Erin's record.

4
00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:13.707
The drift client read her through
SailPoint with two read scopes,

5
00:00:13.707 --> 00:00:17.863
and through Graph with the user read
permission plus the audit permission.

6
00:00:17.863 --> 00:00:21.788
That audit read is the only reason
the help desk write was seen.

7
00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:25.154
In ServiceNow, a finding
writer posted her tasks,

8
00:00:25.154 --> 00:00:29.950
only the verifier may close them,
and an evidence reader may only read.

9
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:35.413
Every call is a row in one file, and a
test fails on any call it doesn't declare.

10
00:00:35.413 --> 00:00:40.556
Why it matters: Gate 1 records the
integration identities, each least-privileged,

11
00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:45.776
with certificates rather than secrets,
and the closer separated from the raisers.

12
00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:48.750
Two details from Chapter
3 are worth carrying.

13
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.611
A SailPoint client-credentials
token has no user context,

14
00:00:52.611 --> 00:00:57.517
and SailPoint warns that most admin
APIs often return forbidden with one,

15
00:00:57.517 --> 00:01:00.975
so Lab 2 records what actually
works in your sandbox.

16
00:01:00.975 --> 00:01:04.942
And the scope that creates
workflows lives on its own client,

17
00:01:04.942 --> 00:01:06.500
used once, then removed.
